        "A portfolio project proves competence better than a transcript line. Scope it to something finishable: one clear problem, three to seven pages or screens, one small database, and one interesting feature done well. A school results checker, a clinic appointment page or a market price board all qualify. In {S}, finished beats ambitious: a small, working, documented project outranks a half-built everything, and recruiters open the link before they read the CV.",
        "Write requirements before code. User stories in the form: as a registrar, I want to search students by level, so I can build lists. They capture actor and value; acceptance criteria say when each story is done. Keep a simple backlog and pull from it in order. In {S}, three well-written stories with criteria produce a better project than thirty vague wishes, because every screen you build then traces to a need someone stated.",
        "Design on paper first: sketch each page, its purpose and its links; draw the ER model for the data, entities, keys, cardinalities; decide the navigation so any page is reachable in two clicks. Choose colours and fonts once, and reuse them. In {S}, the sketch-and-agree step prevents the classic waste of coding three screens users never asked for, and the ER model becomes your database to-do list.",
        "Build the front end with the skeleton you know: semantic HTML5 elements, headings in order, lists, tables for genuinely tabular data, forms with labels. One external CSS file with classes, the box model for spacing, and a media query so the layout survives a 360-pixel phone. Test in the browser as you go, not at the end. In {S}, clean semantic markup is what screen readers, search engines and examiners all reward.",
        "Add interactivity last, in thin slices: validate the form with JavaScript before anything is submitted, then submit to the server with POST, and show a success or error message beside the field. Keep client-side checks as convenience and repeat them on the server as security. In {S}, one working form that saves data and reports errors clearly demonstrates more skill than any carousel of animations.",
        "Create the database from your ER model: tables with proper keys and types, seeded with a few realistic rows. Write the queries the pages need, SELECT for listings, INSERT for forms, UPDATE for edits, and protect them with parameterised queries so user input never becomes executable SQL. In {S}, show one JOIN in the project; a results page joining students to courses is the classic, and it proves the data model actually works.",
        "Test deliberately: click every link, submit empty forms, submit bad data, reload pages mid-flow. Use the browser console for client errors and server logs for the rest; fix, then re-test the path you touched. Show the project to two real users and watch silently where they hesitate. In {S}, the watch-users step finds in five minutes what a week of solo clicking misses, and their first confusion is your navigation bug.",
        "Document as you build: a one-page overview stating problem, users and features; screenshots of each screen; the ER diagram; a short user guide; and a README with setup steps. Prepare a five-minute demo script: problem, live walkthrough, one technical highlight, what you learned. In {S}, the demo script is the presentation, and rehearsing it aloud is the difference between explaining and apologising.",
        "Publish: claim a domain, choose a host that runs your stack, upload files, create and import the database, update configuration with server credentials, and enable HTTPS. Test the live site on a phone with mobile data before announcing it, and keep a local copy under version control. In {S}, a live URL on a CV changes how the project is read: anyone can verify it in ten seconds, anywhere.",
